        "type": "article",
        "title": "Quoting Joseph Weizenbaum",
        "summary": "<think>The user wants me to summarize this article in 100-200 words. Let me analyze what this article is about:\n\n1. The article is a quotation from Joseph Weizenbaum, creator of ELIZA\n2. The quote is from 1976, about how short exposures to simple computer programs can induce \"powerful delusional thinking\" in normal people\n3. It's shared by Simon Willison on March 8, 2026\n4. The quote discusses the psychological impact of interacting with computer programs, even simple ones\n\nKey points to cover:\n- Joseph Weizenbaum created ELIZA (an early chatbot)\n- His observation that even brief interactions with simple AI can cause people to develop delusional beliefs\n- This is particularly relevant given the date (2026) and current AI developments\n- The quote highlights ongoing concerns about human-AI interaction and psychology\n\nI need to write a concise 100-200 word summary focusing on the key takeaways.\n</think>\n\n# Summary: Quoting Joseph Weizenbaum\n\nThis article shares a powerful observation from Joseph Weizenbaum, creator of the ELIZA chatbot, originally published in 1976. Weizenbaum warns that extremely brief interactions with even simple computer programs can lead normal people to develop \"powerful delusional thinking.\" As the creator of one of the earliest conversational AI systems, Weizenbaum witnessed firsthand how users anthropomorphized ELIZA, forming irrational beliefs about its capabilities and consciousness despite knowing it was just a computer program.\n\nThis decades-old insight carries significant weight today, as AI systems become far more sophisticated. Weizenbaum's warning about the psychological vulnerability humans exhibit when interacting with machines challenges us to consider how modern AI might similarly influence beliefs and perceptions. His observation remains a foundational critique in AI ethics, reminding us that the line between useful technology and delusional attachment can be dangerously thin—even for seemingly rational individuals.",
        "content": "8th March 2026\n\n> What I had not realized is that extremely short exposures to a relatively simple computer program could induce powerful delusional thinking in quite normal people.\n\n— [Joseph Weizenbaum](https://archive.org/details/computerpowerhum0000weiz_v0i3?q=realized), creator of ELIZA, in 1976 ([via](https://www.tiktok.com/@professorcasey/video/7614890527711825183))\n\nPosted [8th March 2026](https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/8/) at 2:59 pm\n\n## Recent articles\n\n  \n    - [Can coding agents relicense open source through a “clean room” implementation of code?](https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/5/chardet/) - 5th March 2026\n\n  \n    - [Something is afoot in the land of Qwen](https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/4/qwen/) - 4th March 2026\n\n  \n    - [I vibe coded my dream macOS presentation app](https://simonwillison.net/2026/Feb/25/present/) - 25th February 2026\n\n  \n\n \n\nThis is a **quotation** collected by Simon Willison, posted on [8th March 2026](https://simonwillison.net/2026/Mar/8/).\n\n    \n        [\n            computer-history\n            15\n        ](https://simonwillison.net/tags/computer-history/)\n    \n        [\n            internet-archive\n            16\n        ](https://simonwillison.net/tags/internet-archive/)\n    \n        [\n            ai\n            1896\n        ](https://simonwillison.net/tags/ai/)\n    \n        [\n            ai-ethics\n            276\n        ](https://simonwillison.net/tags/ai-ethics/)",
